---
import { SITE_LINKS } from '@teamtoolbox/registry'
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
---

<Page
  title="Security"
  description="How teamtoolbox keeps your team's data private: end-to-end encryption, automatic deletion and no third-party code in the tools."
>
  <p>
    teamtoolbox is built so that we <strong>can't</strong> read what your team puts into it, rather
    than just promising that we won't. This page explains how, and what it doesn't protect against.
  </p>

  <h2>Two kinds of tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tools that stay in your browser</strong> never send what you enter anywhere. It's
      stored on your device until you export or delete it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shared tools</strong>, like retros, sync between everyone holding the link. Everything
      is encrypted in your browser before it's sent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The link is the key</h2>
  <p>
    When you create a shared room, your browser generates a random 256-bit secret and puts it after
    the <code>#</code> in the link. Browsers never send that part of a link to any server, so it
    doesn't appear in our requests, logs or anyone's referrer headers.
  </p>
  <p>
    From that secret, your browser derives the room's ID (which our server sees) and an encryption
    key (which it never does). Every card, vote, name and comment is encrypted with AES-256-GCM
    before it leaves your device. The encryption is tied to the specific room and item, so it can't
    be moved or altered without detection.
  </p>

  <h2>What our servers can and can't see</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Data</th><th>Visible to us or our host?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cards, votes, comments, names, settings</td><td>No, encrypted in your browser</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The secret in the link</td><td>No, never sent</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The room's ID, which tool it's for, when it expires</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How many items a room has, their rough size and timing</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Your IP address and browser type</td>
        <td>Our host, Cloudflare, processes them to deliver the site. We don't log or store them.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Everything expires</h2>
  <p>
    Each room has a fixed lifetime chosen when it's created, up to 90 days. When it ends, the room
    deletes everything it stored. Anyone in the room can also wipe it immediately. Room data is
    stored in the EU.
  </p>

  <h2>No third-party code in the tools</h2>
  <p>
    Tool pages load only our own code and talk only to our own servers. The small ad in each tool is
    plain text and an image that our code displays. It's not an advertiser's script, so it can't see
    anything on the page. A strict Content Security Policy enforces this in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>What this doesn't protect against</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Anyone who has the link.</strong> Share it only with your team.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>A compromised version of our code.</strong> This is true of every web app that
      encrypts in the browser. We deploy only from our public repository, so changes are visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Metadata</strong> such as when a room is used and how many items it holds.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A compromised device</strong> belonging to someone in the room.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="verify">Check it yourself</h2>
  <p>
    teamtoolbox is open source under the AGPL-3.0 license, so all of the above can be checked
    against the code. <a href={SITE_LINKS.source}>Read the source on GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    Please report security issues privately, rather than in a public issue.
    Use <a href={`${SITE_LINKS.source}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub's private vulnerability reporting</a>.
    If you can't use GitHub, email <a href="mailto:security@teamtoolbox.org">security@teamtoolbox.org</a>.
    We'll acknowledge reports as quickly as we can.
  </p>
</Page>
